    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Seiya Suzuki** leads our lineup with a 67.36 efficiency score, buoyed by an excellent matchup against Seth Lugo and a favorable 16% SP skill advantage. **Corbin Carroll** (64.88) and **Brice Turang** (64.10) remain essential starts despite challenging opposing starters in Yamamoto and Bradley; both profiles are significantly elevated by their top-of-the-order roles and high-upside speed profiles, which are vital given our scarcity-driven strategy. Meanwhile, **Isaac Paredes** (64.27) is a strong utilization play, benefiting from a high platoon ceiling against Michael King, while **Max Muncy** (62.89) gains unexpected value from wind-aided conditions and a favorable platoon matchup that overrides his lower batting order placement.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Zach McKinstry**, who narrowly missed the cut with a 42.34 score. While the wind-out factor provided a marginal boost, his status as a #9 hitter coupled with an unfavorable platoon split against Landen Roupp relegated him to the pine in favor of the higher-ceiling daily starters. Risks abound today, particularly with **Gabriel Moreno** and **Austin Riley** facing the elite arms of Yamamoto and Gerrit Cole, respectively. Furthermore, while **Mickey Moniak** projects as a starter, his 15% penalty due to the platoon disadvantage against Matthew Liberatore makes him our most volatile asset; we will need to monitor the official lineups closely to ensure **Mickey Moniak** isn\u2019t pinched late if a high-leverage bullpen arm emerges. We are currently navigating these matchups while remaining without the services of **James Wood** and **Giancarlo Stanton**, both of whom remain sidelined on the IL.",
